portfolio = head("Hamza Afzal | Backend Engineering", "A backend engineering portfolio with a tested API, reproducible research, and explicit evidence.", "portfolio") + f'''<main id="main"><section class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">BACKEND DEVELOPMENT & ENGINEERING</p><h1>Clear contracts.<br>Tested behavior.<br>Useful decisions.</h1><p class="lede">I'm Hamza Afzal. This project demonstrates one skill: building a tested backend API that turns anonymized search signals into explainable content-review priorities.</p><p><a class="button" href="{ISSUE}">Invite me to an interview ↗</a> <a class="button ghost" href="#work">Inspect the work ↓</a></p><p class="fine">For a backend engineering lead evaluating an intern. Interview requests open a GitHub issue form; nothing is sent automatically.</p></section><div class="portfolio-main"><section id="work" class="case"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / BACKEND CASE STUDY</p><h2>Search signals in.<br>Review priorities out.</h2><h3>The problem</h3><p>A review queue needs a clear request contract, stable ranking, and useful reasons. Invalid signals should fail predictably, and source data should stay out of responses.</p><h3>What I built with AI assistance</h3><p>A dependency-free Python REST API with strict schemas, bounded requests, deterministic tie handling, structured errors, and a documented OpenAPI contract. The POST endpoint uses a transparent fixed rule; the ML study is a separate notebook.</p><h3>What came of it</h3><p>A runnable backend proof with real HTTP integration tests. The ranking endpoint works independently of the research outputs; missing aggregate metrics produce a documented 503 response.</p><p><a href="{REPO}/tree/main/work/backend_api">Review the backend source ↗</a> · <a href="downloads/openapi.json">OpenAPI contract ↗</a></p></div><div><div class="architecture" aria-label="Request flow"><div>01 → Validate request<br><small>1–50 synthetic page IDs · finite bounded signals</small></div><div>02 → Apply explicit rule<br><small>Exposure · freshness · CTR opportunity · depth</small></div><div>03 → Return a stable queue<br><small>Eligibility · score · rank · reason codes</small></div></div><div class="demo"><h3>Try the real local API</h3><p class="fine">Start <code>python work/backend_api/server.py</code> and serve this site locally at port 8000. This button calls the Python server on loopback. The public paper does not host that server.</p><label for="api-payload" class="fine">Synthetic request JSON</label><textarea id="api-payload" spellcheck="false">{html.escape(json.dumps(sample,indent=2))}</textarea><button id="api-run" class="button">Send local API request</button><pre id="api-output" aria-live="polite">Ready. Start the backend and open the local site to run this demonstration.</pre><p class="fine"><a href="downloads/server.py" download>Download server.py</a> · <a href="downloads/test_api.py" download>Download integration tests</a></p></div></div></section><section class="case"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / RESEARCH CASE STUDY</p><h2>Measure the result.<br>Keep the limits visible.</h2><h3>The problem</h3><p>Simple review heuristics may miss patterns in the data. A model comparison needs held-out clients, a shared baseline split, and explicit leakage checks.</p><h3>What I did with AI assistance</h3><p>Used the public-safe FlyRank starter snapshot to compare a fixed baseline with two model candidates. Selected logistic regression using validation average precision, then measured it on eight untouched client groups.</p><h3>What came of it</h3><p>Top-50 precision rose from {base['precision_at_50']:.0%} to {model['precision_at_50']:.0%} on the held-out split. ROC AUC was only {model['roc_auc']:.3f}; same-window features and an observed proxy label limit what this proves.</p><a class="button ghost" href="index.html">Read the complete paper →</a></div><div><img src="assets/capstone_review_budget.png" alt="Held-out precision at different review budgets for the model and baseline" style="width:100%;height:auto"><div class="proof-grid"><div><strong>4,135</strong><span class="fine">held-out pages</span></div><div><strong>0</strong><span class="fine">client overlap</span></div><div><strong>{model['average_precision']:.3f}</strong><span class="fine">average precision</span></div></div></div></section><section id="about" class="case"><div><p class="eyebrow">ABOUT</p><h2>Make the behavior<br>easy to inspect.</h2><p>My focus is Backend Development and Engineering. My recurring work includes API coding, request-schema design, database design, debugging, testing, planning, refactoring, architecture decisions, and checking finished work.</p><p>This portfolio records an AI-assisted internship project. The code, executed notebooks, methods, and limitations are available so a reviewer can assess the actual artifact.</p></div><div><h3>Continue the conversation</h3><p>If you're a backend engineering lead looking for an intern, use the interview-request link to start a conversation about the API and its tradeoffs.</p><p><a class="button" href="{ISSUE}">Invite me to an interview ↗</a></p><p><a href="https://github.com/Hamza2-2">GitHub · Hamza2-2 ↗</a></p><p class="fine">Next case planned: warehouse-backed refresh opportunity validation. It will use the same problem → work → outcome structure after approved data access.</p></div></section></div></main><footer class="footer"><span>Hamza Afzal · FlyRank AI Internship · 2026</span><a href="index.html">Research, methods, and data credit →</a></footer><script src="assets/portfolio.js" defer></script></body></html>'''
portfolio = portfolio.replace("Next case planned: warehouse-backed refresh opportunity validation. It will use the same problem → work → outcome structure after approved data access.", "Next case planned: deploy and harden the backend review API, with HTTPS, authentication, rate limits, tests, rollback, and actual deployment evidence. It will use the same problem → work → outcome structure.")
(DIST / "portfolio.html").write_text(portfolio, encoding="utf-8")
